<Aside type="tip" title="Platform standard">
Enterprise Android applications use feature-oriented Clean Architecture and unidirectional data flow. Compose renders state, ViewModels coordinate user actions, use cases expose domain operations, and repositories coordinate persistence and network access. For persisted profile data, Room is the source observed by the UI.
</Aside>

## How to use this guide

1. Review the layer boundaries and data flow.
2. Follow the user-profile example across the domain, data, presentation, and DI tabs.
3. Apply the review checklist before opening a pull request.

**Scope:** The examples assume an existing Android application with Compose, lifecycle integration, Kotlin serialization, Room, Retrofit, and Hilt configured. They illustrate a feature, not a standalone application. Application setup, Gradle configuration, Retrofit converters, Room database creation, API/DAO providers, navigation, string resources, and the enterprise theme must be supplied by the host project.

**Platform policy versus Android guidance:** This platform requires use cases between ViewModels and repositories. Android's general guidance makes the domain layer optional; the stricter rule here is an internal convention. See [Android architecture recommendations](https://developer.android.com/topic/architecture/recommendations).

