## Clean Architecture & Data Flow

### Layer boundaries

| Layer | Owns | Must not depend on |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| Presentation | Compose UI, screen state, user actions, ViewModels | Retrofit, Room, repository implementations |
| Domain | Business models, use cases, repository contracts | Android framework, Compose, data implementations |
| Data | Repository implementations, DTOs, entities, persistence and API mapping | Presentation |
| Composition root / DI | Object construction and interface bindings | Business decisions |

The domain examples use `javax.inject` constructor annotations and coroutines. “Pure Kotlin” here means no Android framework dependency, rather than no library dependencies.

### Observe locally; refresh remotely

<MermaidZoom title="User profile data flow">

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    UI[Compose screen] -->|Actions| VM[ViewModel]
    VM --> UC[Domain use case]
    UC --> Contract[Repository contract]
    Impl[Repository implementation] -.->|Implements| Contract
    Impl -->|Fetch on refresh| API[Retrofit API]
    API -->|Validated response| Impl
    Impl -->|Persist| DB[Room database]
    DB -->|Observed records| Impl
    Impl -->|Domain flow through contract| UC
    UC -->|Profile updates| VM
    VM -->|Immutable UI state| UI
```

</MermaidZoom>

1. The ViewModel starts observing the requested profile through the use case.
2. Room emits the cached profile, or `null` when no profile is stored.
3. A refresh requests the API and persists a valid response in Room.
4. Room emits the updated record; the ViewModel updates screen state.
5. If refresh fails, cached content remains visible with a retry message. Failure is not reported as a successful refresh.

This follows the local-source observation model in [Android's offline-first guidance](https://developer.android.com/topic/architecture/data-layer/offline-first). The device database is the source of truth for reads in this example; the server still owns the remote profile.

**Failure policy:** Transport failures and server errors preserve cached content. Authentication failures and missing profiles remain explicit failures; the host application's access and cache-invalidation policy must decide whether cached data may remain visible. Partition cached profiles by account and clear protected data on logout or access revocation. This example does not implement those account lifecycle policies, background synchronization, retry backoff, or circuit breaking.

