## Integration requirements

Before adopting the sample:

- Configure `@HiltAndroidApp`, an `@AndroidEntryPoint` host, and providers for `UserApiService` and `UserDao`.
- Register `UserEntity` in your Room database and supply tested migrations.
- Configure Retrofit's serialization converter, base URL, authentication, and error handling through the approved shared SDKs.
- Install `UserFeatureModule` in `SingletonComponent`. This binding has application scope, despite being declared in the feature package.
- Move `IoDispatcher` and `CoroutinesModule` into separate files, preferably in shared DI infrastructure. Do not declare duplicate qualifiers or dispatcher bindings across features.
- Use project-managed dependency versions; the snippets intentionally do not prescribe version numbers.
- Replace example UI strings with string resources, apply the enterprise theme, and add accessibility and localization checks.
- Inject a clock if refresh timestamps are asserted in tests. `cachedAt` records persistence time, not server-side modification time.

The screen keeps profile content visible during refresh. Retry uses the ViewModel's current user ID rather than constructing an empty identifier. Repeated initial loads for the same ID are ignored; switching profiles cancels the previous observation and refresh.

The refresh boundary returns failures as `Result` and rethrows coroutine cancellation. Production error mapping should distinguish authentication, authorization, missing records, transport failures, and persistence failures without exposing raw exception messages to users. Avoid unlimited retries or treating every HTTP failure as an offline success.

## Enterprise SDKs

Use the centralized [Master SDK Catalog](/mobile/platforms/master-sdk-catalog/) for approved `com.enterprise.mobile:*` artifacts, installation instructions, and supported versions. Confirm that this route matches the documentation deployment base before publishing. No release numbers are repeated here because they were not supplied in the source document.

## Patterns to avoid

| Avoid | Use instead |
| :--- | :--- |
| Holding Activity, View, or lifecycle-bound Context references in a ViewModel | Inject a focused abstraction backed by application-scoped infrastructure when needed. |
| Exposing `MutableStateFlow` to the UI | Expose `StateFlow` with `asStateFlow()` and handle actions in the ViewModel. |
| Collecting Android UI flows without lifecycle awareness | Use `collectAsStateWithLifecycle()` in Compose routes. Ordinary `collectAsState()` does not automatically stop collection when the Android lifecycle becomes inactive; this is not inherently a memory leak. |
| Calling Retrofit or Room from composables | Route actions through the ViewModel, use case, and repository boundaries. |
| Importing Compose rendering APIs into ViewModels | Keep presentation rendering in composables and expose immutable state. |
| Replacing cached content with a full-screen spinner on every refresh | Represent content, progress, and refresh errors independently. |
| Swallowing `CancellationException` | Rethrow it so structured cancellation can complete. |
| Adding `@Immutable` to hide unstable or mutable models | Honor the annotation's contract; optimize only after measurement. |
| Defaulting required API fields to empty values | Match the API contract and fail explicitly when required fields are missing. |

## Verification and merge checklist

The source specified both 80% and 90% coverage thresholds. Use the repository's configured CI threshold as the authoritative requirement until the platform owner resolves that conflict; this guide does not introduce a new percentage.

- [ ] Feature code follows the documented layer boundaries; Gradle modules enforce dependencies where applicable.
- [ ] Domain code has no Android framework, Compose, Room, or Retrofit dependencies.
- [ ] Room observation is the only profile data path to the UI; successful refreshes persist before data is displayed.
- [ ] Refresh preserves cached content and surfaces failure without reporting a successful sync.
- [ ] Retry preserves the current user ID, including after initial load failure.
- [ ] Switching users cancels previous work and does not display another user's profile.
- [ ] Cancellation propagates through the repository and use case.
- [ ] Repository tests cover success, empty response, mismatched ID, HTTP errors, transport errors, and persistence failures.
- [ ] ViewModel tests cover cached data, refresh progress, retry, observation failure, blank IDs, and profile switching.
